{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9dfffee-93ab-5b9a-91d2-b8ba63f7d71c" :question/math-academy-id "q-277802" :question/problem "From **top to bottom,** the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $9{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}=36$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand\n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2} & =36 \\\\ 9{y}^{2}-4(0)^{2} & =36 \\\\ 9{y}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,2)$ and $(0,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a53010-eddc-5115-8c70-0f32d0144384" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Hyperbola and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "730c0f83-755b-5070-89cf-87b3254ec271" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264782} {:answer/id #uuid "bae71417-2834-58a6-943c-7f0ad123769b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "730c0f83-755b-5070-89cf-87b3254ec271" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264782} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e1651f2-d37a-54c2-9114-eef3e43f2e83" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264781} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3be5a155-b40c-5e7a-b681-207b7fa85dcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3be5a155-b40c-5e7a-b681-207b7fa85dcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264785} :answer-field/id #uuid "4edf9491-9b0f-5433-9cc4-2aaf28bc63a2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5bea3b57-2a79-53a0-be43-d2e4d30fedc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-278375" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}=1$ intersects the line $y=-x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=-x,$ we first replace $y$ with $-x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(-x)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the $x$-coordinates of the points of intersection are\n$x=-4$ and $x=4.$"}]}
